 # ABA PayWay Payment Link
 
-## Quick Start
+A payment link is a hosted checkout URL you generate via API and share with a
+customer (SMS / email / chat). The customer opens it, pays, and PayWay pushes a
+callback to your `return_url`. Requires `publicKeyPem` (RSA) — the SDK validates
+the PEM before any call and throws `PayWayConfigError` otherwise (use the
+exported `isValidPublicKeyPem()` to pre-flight).
+
+## Quick Start (SDK)
 ```ts
-const link = await payway.paymentLink.create({ title: 'Invoice 123', amount: 10, merchantRefNo: 'invoice-123', returnUrl: 'https://merchant.example/paid', currency: 'USD' });
+const link = await payway.paymentLink.create({
+  title: 'Invoice 123',
+  amount: 9.87,
+  currency: 'USD',
+  merchantRefNo: 'inv-123',
+  returnUrl: 'https://merchant.example/paid',   // POST callback target
+  // paymentLimit: 1,        // 1 = single-use · omit = unlimited · N = up to N payments
+  // expiredDate: 0,         // 0/omit = no expiry · epoch seconds = expires then
+  // payout: [{ acc: '122...', amt: 9.87 }],   // split payout (sum must equal amount)
+});
+console.log(link.data?.payment_link, link.data?.id);
 ```
 
-Payment Link APIs require `publicKeyPem` for RSA-encrypted merchant authorization. The SDK validates the PEM structure before any call — a malformed key throws a clear `PayWayConfigError` (use the exported `isValidPublicKeyPem()` to pre-flight).
+## Parameters (all live inside RSA-encrypted `merchant_auth`)
+| Param | Type | Behavior |
+|---|---|---|
+| `title` | string (≤250) | Link title. **Required.** |
+| `amount` | number | Link amount. Min **0.01 USD** or **100 KHR**. **Required.** |
+| `currency` | `USD` \| `KHR` | **Required.** `PTL99` if unsupported. |
+| `description` | string (≤250) | Optional. |
+| `merchant_ref_no` | string (≤50) | Your reference; echoed in the callback. PayWay does **NOT** reject duplicates — enforce uniqueness yourself. |
+| `return_url` | string (Base64) | **Required.** POST callback target. SDK base64-encodes automatically. `PTL04` if omitted. |
+| `payment_limit` | number | **Controls how many times the link can be paid** — see below. |
+| `expired_date` | epoch seconds | **Controls validity** — see below. `0`/null = no expiry. |
+| `payout` | `[{acc, amt}]` | Optional split payout; sum of `amt` MUST equal `amount`. |
+| `image` | file (≤3MB, JPG/PNG) | Optional display image. |
 
-## Error Handling
-```ts
-import { PayWayConfigError } from 'aba-payway-ts';
-try { await payway.paymentLink.getDetails('link-id'); }
-catch (error) { if (error instanceof PayWayConfigError) console.error(error.message); }
+### Payout (split) — wire format & gotchas
+- The SDK takes `payout: [{ acc, amt, accName? }]` with **numeric** `amt` and
+  serializes each `amt` to a **string** on the wire (e.g. `"5.00"`), matching
+  PayWay's expected `merchant_auth` shape. `acc` is the **ABA account number or
+  MID** (numeric) — never an API credential id. `accName` is optional.
+- The SDK **omits** `payout` entirely when not supplied (it must never be sent as
+  `null`/empty — that triggers "Payout Info is invalid.").
+- `payout` is part of the **RSA-encrypted `merchant_auth`** and is therefore
+  automatically included when the HMAC hash is computed — do not sign the request
+  as if payout were absent.
+- List **every** beneficiary including the merchant's own share; the sum of all
+  `amt` must equal the link `amount` or you get an amount-mismatch error.
+
+## Behavior: payment_limit (how many payments)
+- **omitted / undefined** → **Unlimited**: anyone can pay repeatedly until it expires.
+- **`1`** → **Single-use**: link auto-closes after the first successful payment.
+- **`N` (>1)** → **Multi-use**: accepts up to N payments, then closes.
+- Track usage with `payment_link.total_trxn` (created = `0`). Status flips to
+  CLOSED when `total_trxn` reaches `payment_limit`.
+
+## Behavior: expired_date (validity)
+- **omitted / `0` / null** → **No expiry**: valid until manually closed or paid out.
+- **epoch seconds** (e.g. `Math.floor(Date.now()/1000) + 86400`) → stops accepting
+  payments at that time. Read it back as `data.expired_date` (0 = never).
+
+## Callback (return_url)
+After each successful payment PayWay **POSTs** `Content-Type: application/json`:
+```json
+{ "tran_id": "123456789", "status": "00", "merchant_ref_no": "inv-123" }
+```
+Use `tran_id` with `checkTransaction` / `getTransactionDetail` to fetch the full
+result. Verify authenticity with `payway.verifyCallback(body, hash)` (HMAC-SHA512,
+sorted keys). The POST body field order is arbitrary — the SDK's verifier sorts
+keys before hashing, so do the same on the server side.
+
+## Error codes (status.code)
+| Code | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `PTL02` | Wrong Hash (also: forgot to include payout in the signature / wrong key order) |
+| `PTL04` | Parameter invalid/missing (amount, currency, return_url…) |
+| `PTL05` | Parameter invalid format |
+| `PTL99` | Invalid currency |
+| `PTL132` | Invalid payment link (detail/get) |
+| `PTL-PAYOUT-36` | Payout amount mismatch — sum of beneficiary `amt` ≠ link amount |
+| `PTL146` / `PTL-PAYOUT-37` / `PTL46` | Beneficiary not whitelisted (or wrong type: ABA account vs MID-only profile) |
+| `PTL-RSA-NOTFOUND` | Wrong/old RSA public key used for `merchant_auth` encryption |
+| `Payout Info is invalid.` | Empty array, malformed, or incorrectly encoded payout |
+
+## CLI Equivalent
+```bash
+# Single-use, USD 9.87, no expiry, with a callback URL
+payway-sdk payment-link create \
+  --title "Order 9.87 USD" -a 9.87 -c USD \
+  -r "PL-987-$(date +%s)" --return-url "https://merchant.example/paid"
+
+# Multi-use (up to 5), expires in 1 hour, with split payout
+payway-sdk payment-link create --title "Fundraiser" -a 10 -r ref1 \
+  --return-url "https://merchant.example/paid" \
+  --payment-limit 5 --expired-date "$(($(date +%s)+3600))" \
+  --payout '[{"acc":"122092016015926","amt":6},{"acc":"122091511120425","amt":4}]'
+
+# Inspect (Link ID from create output)
+payway-sdk payment-link detail -i "<data.id>"
 ```
+The CLI interprets `payment_limit`/`total_trxn` (e.g. "3 of 5 (2 remaining)") and
+`expired_date` ("No expiry" vs absolute time) for you.
 
